Your approach is actually the correct one. Don't try to put everything into a single table/model. Instead, separate entities based on what they represent.

1. Hotel and Branch (Recommended)

Suppose your hotel chain is Taj Hotels.

It has branches like:

Taj Mumbai
Taj Delhi
Taj Goa
Taj Jaipur

Then your models should look like this:

Hotel

This stores information common to the entire hotel chain.

Hotel
------
id
name
description
logo
website
email
phone
createdAt
updatedAt

Example

id	name
1	Taj Hotels
HotelBranch

Each branch belongs to one hotel.

HotelBranch
------------
id
hotelId (FK -> Hotel.id)
branchName
address
city
state
country
latitude
longitude
phone
email
ratingAverage
totalReviews
createdAt
updatedAt

Example

id	hotelId	branchName
1	1	Taj Mumbai
2	1	Taj Delhi
3	1	Taj Goa

Relationship

Hotel
  |
  | 1
  |
  |------< HotelBranch (Many)

This is perfect database normalization.

2. Booking

Booking should belong to a branch, not the hotel.

Because people book a specific location.

Booking
--------
id
userId
branchId
checkIn
checkOut
roomType
numberOfGuests
status
totalAmount
createdAt

Relationship

Hotel
   |
   |
HotelBranch
      |
      |
   Booking
3. Reviews

Yes.

Reviews should be a separate model.

Never store reviews inside Hotel or Branch.

Create

Review
-------
id
userId
branchId
bookingId (optional but recommended)
rating
title
comment
createdAt
updatedAt

Relationship

User
  |
  |
Review
  |
  |
HotelBranch
Why review belongs to Branch?

Imagine

Taj Mumbai
⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Taj Delhi
⭐⭐⭐

Taj Goa
⭐⭐⭐⭐

If reviews are attached to the Hotel instead of the Branch, all branches would show the same rating, which is incorrect.

So reviews should be linked to the branch.

4. Room

Rooms also belong to a branch.

Room
-----
id
branchId
roomNumber
roomType
price
capacity
isAvailable

Relationship

Hotel
   |
HotelBranch
      |
      |
    Room
5. Images

Instead of adding many image columns, create a separate table.

HotelImage
-----------
id
branchId
imageUrl
imageType

Example

Lobby
Room
Swimming Pool
Restaurant
Reception
6. Amenities

Again, separate model.

Amenity
---------
id
name
icon

Examples

WiFi
Parking
Pool
Spa
Gym
Restaurant

Since many branches can share the same amenities, use a junction table:

BranchAmenity
--------------
id
branchId
amenityId

This is a many-to-many relationship.

Overall Database Design
Hotel
------
id
name
logo

        1
        |
        |
        | Many
HotelBranch
------------
id
hotelId
name
city
address

      |
      | 1
      |
      | Many
+-------------+
|             |
Room       Review
 |            |
 |            |
Booking      User

HotelBranch
     |
     |
HotelImage

HotelBranch
     |
BranchAmenity
     |
Amenity
One suggestion

Instead of naming the second model HotelModel, consider naming it something like HotelBranch or Branch. That makes the intent much clearer:

Hotel → Information about the hotel chain/brand.
HotelBranch → Information about each physical location.

This design is scalable and is commonly used in booking systems. If later you need features like room availability, payments, offers, wishlists, or staff management, this structure will support them without major changes.